  "subtitle": "A hidden maze in a 4-bit chip, mapped one touch at a time",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's purest hidden-state game: a TMS1100-family microcontroller secretly generates a 50-wall maze, a dragon, and the treasure every game, and the only interface is an 8x8 touch grid of membrane switches under diecast metal miniatures and a beeper with twelve audio cues. There is no display. You learn the walls by blundering into them, you map them with orange plastic markers, and the dragon's position is invisible until it attacks you. Embodied, adversarial, sound-only computing in 1980 — the same family of machine-as-other as Merlin and Stop Thief, but stranger: the computer is a dungeon master who only beeps.",
